 - the requesting scope, derived by the wallet
 - a selection: either explicit references to own final coins, or a map of token type to amount, in which case the
   wallet selects final, unpinned coins covering each amount using its ordinary coin selection
-- a policy: `expires_at`, optional `tag` and `note`
+- a policy: `mode`, `expires_at`, optional `tag` and `note`
 
 Steps:
 
@@ -938,7 +950,9 @@ restart. If the persisted hold state cannot be read or validated, the wallet MUS
 treat the coins as unpinned.
 
 A wallet MAY require user confirmation for creating a hold, exactly as it may for any spend; a hold is a commitment of
-funds and SHOULD be presented as such (amount, token type, requesting scope, expiry).
+funds and SHOULD be presented as such (amount, token type, requesting scope, mode, expiry). Confirmation is given once,
+at creation, and covers every build the hold's mode allows - in particular a `multi` hold does not prompt again when
+the scope rebuilds a stale candidate or builds a sibling.
 
 #### `detach_transaction`
 
@@ -952,8 +966,9 @@ Hands a transaction built with an authorization off to the hold's scope as a can
 4. Any unpinned coin the build had to add is already pinned in the hold (see `spend`); verify this invariant
 5. Persist atomically, then return the serialized transaction to the scope
 
-After `detach_transaction` the same coins can be used again by another authorized build for the same hold, which is
-how several mutually exclusive candidates are produced from one set of coins. The wallet SHOULD warn the user, when
+After `detach_transaction` the same coins can be used again by another authorized build for the same hold if its mode
+is `multi`, which is how several mutually exclusive candidates are produced from one set of coins; a `single` hold
+accepts a new build only once its live candidate is no longer `detached`. The wallet SHOULD warn the user, when
 presenting a hold, that candidates are outside the wallet's control: they may be submitted by the scope at any time
 before they become stale, and local release does not revoke them.
 
@@ -1318,27 +1333,29 @@ indicated.
 A held offer is a swap prepared from a hold, so that the wallet can hand it off and still keep the coins reserved. It
 is the building block of the bidding use cases described in [Holds and pinned coins](#holds-and-pinned-coins). Given a
 hold `H` (created with `hold`, or created implicitly by this operation when the caller asks for a fresh one), the
-token amounts to provide and the token amounts to receive:
+token amounts to provide, the token amounts to receive, and whether fees are to be paid:
 
 1. Create expected outputs for each token type to be received, addressed to the wallet itself. The wallet MAY have
    never held that token type (e.g. a token that will be minted by the settling contract); an output does not require
-   prior ownership of its token type.
-2. Combine them into a guaranteed shielded offer skeleton with target imbalances equal to the amounts to provide.
-3. Balance the skeleton with `spend` authorized by `H` and with fee payment disabled: inputs come from the pinned
-   coins of `H`, change is addressed to the wallet.
-4. Prove and bind. The result is a transaction consisting of a single guaranteed shielded offer, mergeable by any
-   party.
-5. `detach_transaction` it into `H` and return it to the caller.
-
-Repeating steps 1-5 with different target imbalances or recipients produces sibling candidates from the same coins.
-Because each candidate's input proofs are bound to the commitment-tree root at build time, candidates must be rebuilt
-when the root leaves the ledger's retention window; the caller SHOULD be told the validity bound together with the
-candidate.
-
-Fees are intentionally not covered by a held offer: the party that merges the candidates into the settlement
-transaction balances it and pays Dust. A held offer that must also carry Dust would need an intent with a Dust spend,
-whose timing constraints (Dust grace period) are much tighter than the hold; such offers are out of scope of this
-stage.
+   prior ownership of its token type. Shielded outputs go into a guaranteed shielded offer skeleton; unshielded outputs
+   go into an unshielded offer skeleton of a new intent with a randomly chosen segment id (see
+   [Prepare a swap](#prepare-a-swap)).
+2. Set target imbalances equal to the amounts to provide, per token kind.
+3. Balance the skeletons with `spend` authorized by `H`: shielded inputs come from `H`'s pinned shielded coins,
+   unshielded inputs from `H`'s pinned UTxOs, change is addressed to the wallet. If fees are to be paid, Dust spends
+   are created from `H`'s pinned Dust outputs first ([Creating a Dust spend](#creating-a-dust-spend)) and placed in
+   the intent. Unshielded inputs and Dust spends require an intent; a purely shielded offer without fees needs none.
+4. Prove, sign the intent (if any) and bind. The result is a transaction whose guaranteed shielded offer and whose
+   intent (if any) are mergeable as a unit by any party: shielded offers merge by set union, intents merge by segment
+   id.
+5. `detach_transaction` it into `H` and return it to the caller together with its validity bound.
+
+Repeating steps 1-5 with different amounts or recipients on a `multi` hold produces sibling candidates from the same
+coins. The validity bound of a candidate is the minimum of: the root retention window for its shielded and Dust inputs
+(counted from build time), and the TTL of its intent if it has one. The caller SHOULD schedule rebuilds before the
+bound passes. Fees are a choice of the use case: when the settling party pays, the candidate is built without fees and
+without an intent; when the bidder pays, the candidate carries a Dust spend and the fee amount is fixed at build time,
+so the settling party MUST still cover any shortfall of the merged transaction.
 
